Dijkstra! One of the all-time greats — invented in 20 minutes at a café, allegedly without paper, which is the most demoralizing origin story in computer science. The core idea, stripped bare: it's a controlled flood. You know the shortest distance to the start (zero, you're standing there). At every step, you take the UNVISITED node with the smallest known distance, declare it final ('nothing can ever reach you cheaper — I've already checked everything cheaper'), and use it to improve the distance estimates of its neighbors. Repeat until you've finalized the goal. The magic guarantee: because you always expand the globally cheapest frontier node, the first time you finalize any node, you've found its true shortest path — no takebacks needed. (This is also exactly why negative edge weights break it: a negative edge could create a cheaper route to an already-finalized node, violating the no-takebacks promise. Games rarely have negative walking costs, so you're safe.) The implementation skeleton for your assignment:

import heapq

def dijkstra(graph, start, goal):
    dist = {start: 0}
    prev = {}
    pq = [(0, start)]          # (distance, node)
    visited = set()
    while pq:
        d, node = heapq.heappop(pq)
        if node in visited:
            continue           # stale entry — skip
        if node == goal:
            break
        visited.add(node)
        for neighbor, weight in graph[node]:
            nd = d + weight
            if nd < dist.get(neighbor, float('inf')):
                dist[neighbor] = nd
                prev[neighbor] = node
                heapq.heappush(pq, (nd, neighbor))
    # reconstruct path by walking prev[] from goal back to start

Implementation notes that separate working from flailing: (1) the 'if node in visited: continue' handles duplicate heap entries — we lazily leave stale entries in the heap instead of decreasing keys, which Python's heapq can't do; this is the standard idiom. (2) prev[] is your breadcrumb trail — reconstruct the path by following it backward from the goal, then reverse. (3) Complexity: O((V+E) log V) with the heap — fine for game maps. Now, the assignment-relevant upgrade you'll want to mention: A*. It's Dijkstra plus a compass — you add a heuristic (like straight-line distance to the goal) to the priority, so the flood flows TOWARD the goal instead of expanding in all directions equally. Same code, one changed line in the heap priority: (nd + heuristic(neighbor, goal), neighbor). With an admissible heuristic (never overestimates), A* stays exactly correct and explores dramatically fewer nodes — for game pathfinding it's the industry answer, and implementing Dijkstra first means A* is a five-minute victory lap. Go flood some mazes — and if the path comes out wrong, print the prev[] chain; the bug is in reconstruction 80% of the time!

My softmax implementation returns NaN for large inputs:

def softmax(x):
    e = np.exp(x)
    return e / e.sum()

Classic numerical landmine — your math is right and your floats are having a breakdown! np.exp(1000) overflows to inf (float64 taps out around exp(709)), and then inf/inf = NaN, and the whole distribution dissolves into regret. The fix is the max-subtraction trick, used by every framework ever:

def softmax(x):
    z = x - np.max(x)
    e = np.exp(z)
    return e / e.sum()

Why it's legal: softmax(x) = softmax(x + c) for any constant c — shifting all inputs equally cancels out in the ratio (both numerator and denominator pick up the same e^c factor). Subtracting the max means the largest exponent is exp(0) = 1, so overflow becomes impossible; the worst case for tiny values is underflow to 0, which is harmless in the sum. Two bonus notes while we're here: (1) for multi-dimensional inputs, subtract per-row: z = x - x.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True), and sum with the same axis/keepdims — forgetting keepdims is the sequel bug to this one. (2) If you're feeding this into a log later (cross-entropy!), don't do log(softmax(x)) — use the log-sum-exp trick directly (scipy.special.log_softmax) to dodge a SECOND round of precision loss. Floating point: it's not malicious, it just has boundaries, and exp() sprints toward them faster than anything else in ML. Respect the 709!

First week and you're already writing conditionals — great pace! The bug is sneaky and it catches literally everyone: input() ALWAYS returns text (a string), even when the user types numbers. So age is '25' — the CHARACTERS two-five, not the number twenty-five — and Python refuses to compare a string with a number using >=, because it honestly doesn't know whether '25' should count as bigger than 18 (what would 'hello' >= 18 mean?). The fix is converting the text to a number:

age = int(input('How old are you? '))
if age >= 18:
    print('adult')

int(...) says 'read these characters as a whole number.' That's the whole fix! Bonus armor for week two: if the user types 'twenty' instead of digits, int() will crash with a ValueError — the graceful version wraps it:

try:
    age = int(input('How old are you? '))
except ValueError:
    print('Numbers only, please!')

But don't rush there — for week one, the int() fix is the lesson. The bigger pattern you just met (worth filing away, you'll see it constantly): data has TYPES — text, whole numbers, decimals — and Python keeps them strictly separate, which feels pedantic now and will save you from silent nonsense forever after. Type confusion caught loudly beats type confusion hidden quietly — some languages choose otherwise and their users have stories. Keep going, you're doing great!

